<Project>

  <!--
    Git #3006 - extend the .pnpmfile.cjs metered-connection hard-block (Git #1986) to
    "dotnet restore"/"build"/"publish" run directly against any desktop .csproj (BuildConsole,
    MyArchitect, SuperShopper, WebTester) - a real NuGet-restore download vector #3001's audit
    flagged as ADVISORY-ONLY: BUILD_NETWORK is injected into every launched build session's
    environment, but nothing under desktop/ read it before this file.

    This is deliberately independent of the BuildConsole version-update deploy path's own gate
    (MainWindow.VersionUpdate.cs -> BuildConsoleSettings.CurrentNetworkIsMetered()): that check
    reads the Location setting directly from the RUNNING BuildConsole.exe process, which never
    has BUILD_NETWORK set on itself (only build sessions IT LAUNCHES get that env var) - so this
    target never fires for the deploy script's own "dotnet build" call, only for a build session
    invoking "dotnet" itself.

    Placed in Directory.Build.targets (not .props): it must be imported AFTER the SDK's own
    Sdk.targets so the real "Restore" target already exists to hook BeforeTargets onto. Applies
    to every project under desktop/ via MSBuild's normal upward-directory-search convention -
    no per-project change needed. Verified empirically (BuildConsole.csproj, .NET SDK 8.0.425):
    a plain "dotnet restore BuildConsole.csproj" with BUILD_NETWORK=metered aborts with real exit
    code 1 and a real "error : BLOCKED..." line before any package download; the identical
    command with BUILD_NETWORK unset (or =unmetered) restores normally, exit 0.

    HONEST LIMITS (same as .pnpmfile.cjs's own section, and scripts/dev-server/network-gate.mjs):
    a "dotnet restore" flag that skips dependency evaluation, or an out-of-tree "msbuild"
    invocation that never evaluates this file, or a shell that unsets BUILD_NETWORK before
    invoking dotnet, are not caught here - no MSBuild hook is guaranteed to run for every
    possible invocation, the same residual gap .pnpmfile.cjs already names for its own
    equivalent bypass flag. This stops the reflexive/compliant
    case, not a deliberately-evading one. There is no flag/property a build session can pass to
    grant itself an exception; only Shane's Location toggle / one-shot override in BuildConsole
    clears BUILD_NETWORK for a launched build.
  -->
  <Target Name="RefuseNuGetRestoreWhenMetered" BeforeTargets="Restore;CollectPackageReferences">
    <Error
      Condition=" '$(BUILD_NETWORK)' == 'metered' "
      Text="BLOCKED: this is Shane's capped (metered) connection - BUILD_NETWORK=metered. Refusing: NuGet restore for $(MSBuildProjectName) ($(MSBuildProjectFullPath)). STOP and report this to Shane rather than retrying or working around it. Only Shane clears the metered gate, via the Location toggle / one-shot override in BuildConsole - there is no flag or env var a build session can set to override it (Git #1986 / #3006)." />
  </Target>

</Project>
